\subsection*{1.4 Distance Between Two Points in the 2-D Plane}

We know how to find the distance between two points if they are on the axes or if they form a line segment parallel to the axes. For example, in Fig. 1.5 we can find the distances \(\mathrm{W}_{1} \mathrm{~W}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{S}_{1} \mathrm{~S}_{2}\). What should we do if the segment joining the points is not parallel to either axis? We can use the Baudhāyana-Pythagoras theorem which you have studied in Grade 8. We can use this result to find the distance between any two points in the xy-plane.

Look at triangle ADM in Fig. 1.6.

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[alt={},max width=\textwidth]{https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/4cec7484-b4be-4c71-abd9-c86b8e70e6e7-029.jpg?height=886&width=996&top_left_y=327&top_left_x=349}
\captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
\caption{Fig. 1.6}
\end{figure}

Triangle ADM is an acute angled triangle in the first quadrant. How do we find the lengths of its sides \(\mathrm{AD}, \mathrm{DM}\) and MA ?

\section*{Think and Reflect}
1. In moving from \(\mathrm{A}(3,4)\) to \(\mathrm{D}(7,1)\), what distance has been covered along the x -axis? What about the distance along the y -axis?
2. Can these distances help you find the distance AD?

Fig. 1.7 gives us a clue. The distance moved along the x -axis is given by CD .
CD = x-coordinate of D - x-coordinate of A = 7-3=4.

The distance moved along the y -axis is given by AC .
\[\mathrm{AC}=\mathrm{y} \text {-coordinate of } \mathrm{A}-\mathrm{y} \text {-coordinate of } \mathrm{D}=4-1=3 \text {. }\]

Using the Baudhāyana-Pythagoras Theorem, we get the distance
\[\mathrm{AD}=\sqrt{4^{2}+3^{2}}=5 \text { units. }\]

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[alt={},max width=\textwidth]{https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/4cec7484-b4be-4c71-abd9-c86b8e70e6e7-030.jpg?height=732&width=996&top_left_y=254&top_left_x=349}
\captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
\caption{Fig. 1.7}
\end{figure}

We similarly find the distances DM and MA:
\[\begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{DM}=\sqrt{2^{2}+5^{2}}=\sqrt{29} \text { units. } \\
& \mathrm{MA}=\sqrt{6^{2}+2^{2}}=\sqrt{40} \text { units. }
\end{aligned}\]

In general, the distance between the points ( \(x_{1}, y_{1}\) ) and ( \(x_{2}, y_{2}\) ) is given by
\[\sqrt{\left(x_{2}-x_{1}\right)^{2}+\left(y_{2}-y_{1}\right)^{2}}\]
and is calculated as shown in Fig. 1.8.

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[alt={},max width=\textwidth]{https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/4cec7484-b4be-4c71-abd9-c86b8e70e6e7-030.jpg?height=594&width=855&top_left_y=1521&top_left_x=420}
\captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
\caption{Fig. 1.8}
\end{figure}

It makes no difference whether ( \(x_{2}-x_{1}\) ) and ( \(y_{2}-y_{1}\) ) are positive or negative, as we are simply measuring the shifts along the two axes.

What if, \(x_{1}, x_{2}, y_{1}, y_{2}\) take negative values? In Fig. 1.9, triangle AMD is reflected in the y -axis. What are the coordinates of the images of points \(\mathrm{A}, \mathrm{M}\), and D ?

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[alt={},max width=\textwidth]{https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/4cec7484-b4be-4c71-abd9-c86b8e70e6e7-031.jpg?height=714&width=1305&top_left_y=496&top_left_x=195}
\captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
\caption{Fig. 1.9}
\end{figure}
\[\begin{gathered}
\mathrm{C}^{\prime} \mathrm{D}^{\prime}=\mathrm{x} \text {-coordinate of } \mathrm{A}^{\prime}-\mathrm{x} \text {-coordinate of } \mathrm{D}^{\prime}=-3-(-7)=4 . \\
\mathrm{A}^{\prime} \mathrm{C}^{\prime}=\mathrm{y} \text {-coordinate of } \mathrm{A}^{\prime}-\mathrm{y} \text {-coordinate of } \mathrm{D}^{\prime}=4-1=3 .
\end{gathered}\]

Using the Baudhāyana-Pythagoras Theorem, we get \(\mathrm{AD}=\sqrt{4^{2}+3^{2}}=5\) units.

You can similarly calculate both D'M' and M'A':
\[\begin{aligned}
& D^{\prime} M^{\prime}=\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+5^{2}}=\sqrt{29} \text { units } \\
& M^{\prime} A^{\prime}=\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+2^{2}}=\sqrt{40} \text { units. }
\end{aligned}\]

We see that reflection has preserved the lengths of the sides of the triangles.

