To generalise this pattern, we observe that the number of squares at each stage is one less than twice the number of the term. For example, in Term 2, the number of squares is \(2 \times 2-1=3\), in Term 5, the number of squares is \(2 \times 5-1=9\) and so on.

This leads us to conclude that the number of squares at Stage \(n\) is given by \(2 n-1\).

The polynomial \(2 n-1\) has degree 1 . Hence, it is an example of a linear polynomial. Also, the difference between consecutive terms in the sequence of the number of squares, that is, \(1,3,5,7,9 \ldots\) is the constant value 2. Thus, with each stage, the number of squares increases by 2 . The relationship between the number of the stage and the number of square tiles is a linear relationship.

