\subsection*{2.6 VISUALISING LINEAR RELATIONSHIPS}

As we have seen in many examples in this chapter, a linear pattern or relationship can be expressed in the form of an equation \(y=a x+b\). Now we shall learn to plot such an equation as a straight line.

To plot a linear equation, we need to identify any two points on the line. For example, to plot \(y=2 x+1\), we may identify two points as follows:

When \(x=0, y=1\), so \((0,1)\) is a point on the line. We can call this point A . We say that the x -coordinate of A is 0 and the y -coordinate is 1 . Also, when \(x=3, y=7\). Thus, \(\mathrm{B}(3,7)\) is another point on the line. We plot these points on the coordinate plane, join them and extend the line in both directions as shown in Fig. 2.5.

