\subsection*{3.4.2 The Density of Rational Numbers}

One of the most magical properties of rational numbers is that they are dense. Between any two integers, say 1 and 2 , there is a rational number \(\frac{3}{2}\). Between 1 and \(\frac{3}{2}\), there is another rational number \(\frac{5}{4}\).

No matter how close two rational numbers are on the number line, you can always find another rational number between them by taking their average. For example, a rational number between 1 and \(\frac{3}{2}\) can be found by taking their average: \(\frac{1+\frac{3}{2}}{2}=\frac{5}{4}\). Try to explain why the average of two rational numbers \(a\) and \(b\), which equals \(\frac{(a+b)}{2}\), is always a rational number between \(a\) and \(b\).

