\subsection*{4.4. More Identities}

What will happen if we want to find the square of the sum of three numbers \(a, b\) and \(c\), that is, \((a+b+c)^{2}\) ?

Let us replace \(b+c\) by \(d\).
We already know, \((a+d)^{2}=a^{2}+2 a d+d^{2}\).
Thus, replacing \(d\) by \((b+c)\), we get \(a^{2}+2 a d+d^{2}=a^{2}+2 a(b+c)+(b+c)^{2}\).

So, \((a+b+c)^{2}=a^{2}+2 a b+2 a c+b^{2}+2 b c+c^{2}\).
It may be more convenient to remember
