\subsection*{5.7.1 Angle subtended by an arc at a point on the circle outside the arc}

By 'angle subtended by arc AXB at a point on the circle outside the arc' we mean the angle ACB where C is any point on the circle but not on arc AXB. Remarkably, the measure of this angle does not depend upon which specific point C we pick, so long as it is on the circle and outside the arc!

Activity: Draw a circle and a chord AB . Fix an arc AKB formed by AB and a point K between \(\mathrm{A}, \mathrm{B}\) on the circle. Measure the angle subtended at the centre by arc AKB. Take three points P, Q, R on the circle outside \(\operatorname{arc} \mathrm{AKB}\). Measure the angles subtended by arc AKB at points \(\mathrm{P}, \mathrm{Q}, \mathrm{R}\). What do you notice?

Repeat this activity for a different arc AKB. Based on this activity, we can make a statement about the angles subtended by an arc of a circle at the centre and at a point

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[alt={},max width=\textwidth]{https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/4cec7484-b4be-4c71-abd9-c86b8e70e6e7-127.jpg?height=491&width=484&top_left_y=1331&top_left_x=1041}
\captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
\caption{Fig. 5.20}
\end{figure}
of the circle outside the arc. We shall show that the following is true.

Theorem 9: The angle subtended by an arc at the centre of the circle is double the angle subtended by the arc at any point on the circle outside the arc.

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[alt={},max width=\textwidth]{https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/4cec7484-b4be-4c71-abd9-c86b8e70e6e7-128.jpg?height=461&width=464&top_left_y=481&top_left_x=245}
\captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
\caption{Fig. 5.21: Angle subtended by arc AFB}
\end{figure}

Given: AFB is an arc. \(\angle \mathrm{ACB}\) is the angle subtended by arc AFB at centre C . D is a point on the circle outside arc AFB (see Fig. 5.21).

We will assume for now that D is such that DC when extended cuts the circle at some point E on \(\operatorname{arc} \mathrm{AFB}\), as shown.

We will consider the other case later.

To show: \(\angle \mathrm{BCA}=2 \angle \mathrm{BDA}\).
Why is this true? Join D to C and extend DC to cut the circle at a point E on \(\operatorname{arc} \mathrm{AFB}\). Now \(\triangle \mathrm{DCB}\) is isosceles with \(\mathrm{CB}=\mathrm{CD}\). So, \(\angle \mathrm{CBD}=\angle \mathrm{CDB}\).
\(\angle \mathrm{BCE}\) is the exterior angle of \(\triangle \mathrm{BCD}\). By the exterior angle theorem,
\[\angle \mathrm{BCE}=\angle \mathrm{CBD}+\angle \mathrm{CDB}=2 \angle \mathrm{BDC} .\]

Similarly, \(\triangle \mathrm{ADC}\) is an isosceles triangle with \(\mathrm{CA}=\mathrm{CD}\).
So,
\[\angle \mathrm{CAD}=\angle \mathrm{CDA}\]
\(\angle \mathrm{ACE}\) is the exterior angle of \(\triangle \mathrm{ADC}\). By the exterior angle theorem,
\[\angle \mathrm{ACE}=\angle \mathrm{CAD}+\angle \mathrm{CDA}=2 \angle \mathrm{CDA} .\]

Now \(\angle \mathrm{BCA}=\angle \mathrm{BCE}+\angle \mathrm{ECA}\), and \(\angle \mathrm{BDA}=\angle \mathrm{BDE}+\angle \mathrm{EDA}\).
Hence \(\angle \mathrm{BCA}=2(\angle \mathrm{BDC}+\angle \mathrm{CDA})=2 \angle \mathrm{BDA}\).

So, \(\angle \mathrm{BCA}=2 \angle \mathrm{BDA}\).
The explanation we have given does not work if the situation is as shown in Fig. 5.22, where the extension of DC meets the circle at a point E outside the \(\operatorname{arc} \mathrm{AFB}\). We must rework the explanation for this case.

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[alt={},max width=\textwidth]{https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/4cec7484-b4be-4c71-abd9-c86b8e70e6e7-129.jpg?height=469&width=481&top_left_y=268&top_left_x=239}
\captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
\caption{Fig 5.22: Angle subtended by arc \(A F B\)}
\end{figure}

Given: AFB is an arc. \(\angle \mathrm{ACB}\) is the angle subtended by arc AFB at centre C . D is a point on the circle outside arc AFB such that when we extend DC it cuts the circle at some point E outside the arc AFB, as shown (Fig. 5.22).

We shall again make use of the properties of isosceles triangles.
\(\angle \mathrm{ACE}=\angle \mathrm{ADC}+\angle \mathrm{CAD}=2 \angle \mathrm{ADC}\), since \(\mathrm{CA}=\mathrm{CD}\) (and so \(\angle \mathrm{ADC}=\angle \mathrm{CAD}\) ).
Similarly, \(\angle \mathrm{BCE}=\angle \mathrm{BDC}+\angle \mathrm{CBD}=2 \angle \mathrm{BDC}\).
Also, \(\angle \mathrm{ACB}=\angle \mathrm{ACE}-\angle \mathrm{BCE}\), and \(\angle \mathrm{ADB}=\angle \mathrm{ADC}-\angle \mathrm{BDC}\).
Hence \(\angle \mathrm{ACB}=2 \angle \mathrm{ADB}\).

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[alt={},max width=\textwidth]{https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/4cec7484-b4be-4c71-abd9-c86b8e70e6e7-129.jpg?height=535&width=559&top_left_y=1112&top_left_x=229}
\captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
\caption{Fig. 5.23: Angles subtended by an arc are equal}
\end{figure}

Something very interesting comes out of this. This is what the activity before Theorem 9 suggested. Take an arc AB . Decide how you want to go from A to B along the arc. Take any point D on the circle, outside \(\operatorname{arc} \mathrm{AB}\).

Then, no matter where D is, so long as it is outside the arc and on the circle, \(\angle \mathrm{ADB}\) is the same!

Let us understand this better using Fig. 5.23. Consider the arc of the circle going from A to B via X . The points on the circle outside this arc are points you cross as you go from A to B via E. The angle subtended by AXB at the centre is the angle swept by CA as we move from CA to CB via X . Points \(\mathrm{E}, \mathrm{F}\) and D are all not on AXB . The theorem tells us that \(\angle \mathrm{AEB}=\angle \mathrm{ADB}=\angle \mathrm{AFB}=\frac{1}{2} \angle \mathrm{ACB}\).

Corollary: The angle subtended by a diameter at any point on the circle is \(90^{\circ}\).

Corollary: A corollary is a fact that follows immediately from an already proved result.

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[alt={},max width=\textwidth]{https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/4cec7484-b4be-4c71-abd9-c86b8e70e6e7-130.jpg?height=467&width=502&top_left_y=604&top_left_x=219}
\captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
\caption{Fig. 5.24}
\end{figure}

Why is this true? Let AB be a diameter (Fig. 5.24). We must show that \(\angle \mathrm{ADB}\) is \(90^{\circ}\). The arc from \(A\) to \(B\) we take is the arc not containing D . What is the angle subtended by that arc at C? We have to move from A along that arc till we reach B. So, the angle subtended is \(\angle \mathrm{ACB}\), a straight angle, \(180^{\circ}\). Hence, \(\angle \mathrm{ADB}=\frac{1}{2} \angle \mathrm{ACB}=90^{\circ}\).

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[alt={},max width=\textwidth]{https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/4cec7484-b4be-4c71-abd9-c86b8e70e6e7-130.jpg?height=611&width=460&top_left_y=1153&top_left_x=302}
\captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
\caption{Fig. 5.25: Points and the chord}
\end{figure}

This fact that the angles in the same arc segment are all equal is a beautiful fact that distinguishes the circle from all other shapes.

In Fig. 5.25, the angles that arc AB subtends at the points F , G outside the circle are different. For the points inside the circle, \(\angle \mathrm{AIB}, \angle \mathrm{ACB}\) and \(\angle \mathrm{AHB}\) are all different. But for all points \(P\) on arc \(A B\), the subtended angles are the same. Thus, for points \(\mathrm{D}, \mathrm{E}, \angle \mathrm{ADB}=\angle \mathrm{AEB}\).

