\caption{Base, \(\mathrm{BC}=2 a\) units}
\end{figure}

Fig. 6.22

A median is the line segment joining a vertex of the triangle to the midpoint of the opposite side. In Fig. 6.22, AD is a median of \(\triangle \mathrm{ABC}\).

Consider \(\triangle \mathrm{ABD}\) and \(\triangle \mathrm{ACD}\). Their bases are equal ( \(\mathrm{BD}=\mathrm{DC}\) ), and they have equal height \(h\). Using the formula for the area of a triangle, we see that they have equal area \(\frac{a h}{2}\) sq. units!

We state this as a theorem.
Theorem: A median of a triangle divides it into two triangles with equal area.

Does this come as a surprise? It should! After all, \(\triangle \mathrm{ABD}\) and \(\triangle \mathrm{ACD}\) are (in general) differently shaped (i.e., not congruent to each other). But we have just proved that they have the same area!

